Edge.origin is the pass-id axis, and for the two SCIP arms it is not the
registration name: they register as rust_analyzer / scip_python and their
records carry "scip", because the translation that emits them is shared by
both arms and is a pass in its own right. catalog._SYNTHETIC_PASS_IDS
declares it as one, per ADR-0044, and all_known_pass_ids() resolves it -- so
the axis annotation was never violated.

What was missing is that nothing said so where a consumer could read it.
ADR-0057 §14's origin condition bridged the gap by unioning
RegisteredAnalyzer.backend, which happens to spell "scip" for both arms.
That is the right answer off the wrong field: backend is the PARSING BACKEND
label, and the registry's own docstring says it is "decoupled from pass_id so
the ID stays stable across backend swaps". A backend swap, or a producer
whose two labels differ, would have disarmed the supersession rule with no
error -- the false-negative direction, silent. It also admitted the
incumbent's "ast" as a Python producer token.

RegisteredAnalyzer.emits_origin now states it. None keeps meaning "the
registration name", true for every analyzer but these two, so the
declaration marks the exception instead of adding noise to a hundred rows --
absent is not empty here either.

  anchored_producer_tokens("python") == {python, scip_python, scip}   ("ast" gone)
  anchored_producer_tokens("rust")   == {rust, rust_analyzer, scip}

The declaration is checked against the EMISSION, in each arm's own package:
a recorded-fixture test per backend asserts every symbol and edge the
translation produces carries the declared token, with a non-empty guard so it
cannot pass vacuously. A declaration nothing checks is a claim in a costume,
and this one decides whether §14 recognises the producer at all.

The §14 test fixture registered pyscip with backend="scip" and relied on that
coincidence; it now declares emits_origin, as production does, and
test_the_backend_name_spelling_is_accepted is renamed for what it tests.

NOT asserted: that every producer's emitted origin matches its registration.
Only the two declaring an exception are checked against their emissions, plus
four sentinels that declare none. Making the general case impossible needs a
corpus-wide emission check the recorded fixtures cannot supply.

